{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Margin measurement, and a third finding the measurement forced\n\nRe-measured on i-00880b2c7f13527c5 at 19:03 UTC, load average 3.45 — representative of the\ncondition that produces the kills.\n\n    95.01s rc=124  timeout 95  du -x --block-size=1 --max-depth=1 /var/lib/docker\n    95.01s rc=124  timeout 95  du -x --block-size=1 --max-depth=1 /var/lib/docker\n   125.03s rc=124  timeout 125 du -sx --block-size=1 /var/lib/docker\n   125.04s rc=124  timeout 125 du -sx --block-size=1 /var/lib/docker/overlay2\n\n**The walk does not complete at any bound.** Not at 95s, not at 125s, twice each. Yet it\ncompletes in well under a minute when the cache is warm (breadcrumbs at 17:48, 17:58, 18:03,\n18:09, 18:42). The reading is **bimodal — fast, or unobtainable** — which retires the idea that\na bigger ceiling buys a reading. It buys only a longer stall. `DOCKER_DU_TIMEOUT` therefore\ngoes to **60**, capturing the fast mode and failing fast in the stalled one; §2e already says a\nmeasurement that could not be taken publishes as silence, and `docker_du_seconds` now says why.\n\n### Third finding: the ceilings were never composed, and the real worst case was ~495s\n\nTiming the tail exposed that `du -sx /var/tmp` **also** exhausts its bound (15.06s at a 15s\nprobe), and that led me to inventory every wall-clock bound in the script rather than only the\none I came for:\n\n| bound | ceiling |\n|---|---|\n| 2 x docker root `du` | 120 + 120 |\n| `docker system df` | 20 |\n| `du /var/log/journal` | 60 |\n| `du /var/tmp` | 60 |\n| per-entry `du -sk` loop | 10 x N |\n| `docker ps` + `docker stats` | 15 + 15 |\n| 7 cursor-anchored journal reads | 7 x 10 |\n| `git ls-remote` | 15 |\n\n**Over 495s of ceilings inside a 240s timeout.** Every one carries a comment justifying itself\nagainst that timeout; the journal section literally reasons \"12 scans x 10 s = 120 s < the\n240 s TimeoutStartSec\" while the docker section was separately entitled to 240s of the same\nbudget. Each ceiling is individually correct and the set is individually wrong. **Nobody was\nadding them up** — that is the defect class, and the double `du` was only the instance that\nhappened to hit first.\n\nSo the fix is not \"make the `du` cheaper\" but \"hold the budget in one place\":\n\n- `probe_budget_left` = `PROBE_DEADLINE_SEC - elapsed - PROBE_TAIL_RESERVE_SEC` (240 - 80).\n- `clamped <ceiling> <cmd>` grants `min(ceiling, budget_left)` and every wall-clock-bounded call\n  in the script now goes through it — all 13 of them.\n- A `clamped` call starting at t cannot end after `deadline - reserve`, and **no sequence of\n  them can either**. The clamped portion of a run is bounded at **160s by construction**, not by\n  estimate.\n\n### Margin\n\n- **Guaranteed: 80s** (240 - 160), and it is structural — it does not depend on how slow the\n  disk gets, because a stalled call is granted less, not more.\n- **Measured typical: ~105s elapsed, so ~135s margin.** From the host, at load average 3.45:\n  docker walk <=60, ledger 15, `/var/log/journal` du 0.01s, `/var/tmp` du ~15, `docker ps` 0.03s,\n  `docker stats` 3.22s, `free` 0.02s, three health curls 0.69s total, `git ls-remote` 0.25s,\n  seven journal reads 0.49-1.69s each.\n- Floor of 1s rather than 0 on an exhausted budget, deliberately: a clamped call must still RUN\n  and fail, because \"could not be measured\" is publishable and being SIGKILLed is not.\n\n### Answering criterion 4 — reserve, not reordering\n\nReordering only changes *which* metric starves. A reserve no earlier section can spend means\nnone can. Pinned by a defect-seeded test: the `du` stub is driven 3s past a 2s budget and the\nassertion is that `mem_used_percent` — the metric that went blind for 49 minutes, and which\nsits ~400 lines after the walk — still publishes, and that `probe_ok` still follows it.\n\n### Criterion 3 — truncation is now a signal\n\n`probe_ok` (once, last), `probe_elapsed_seconds`, and `probe_truncated` from an `ExecStopPost`\nhook that systemd runs even after a timeout SIGTERM. Two alarms in `monitoring_9313.tf`:\n`probe_truncated` (notBreaching — ExecStopPost cannot run if the unit never ran) and the\nmissing-data arm of `probe_ok` (breaching — that is the \"no runs at all\" case).\n\n### Tests\n\n12 new tests in `tests/scripts/test_observability_timeout_9313.py`. Verified RED against\n`origin/main`: **10 of the 12 fail** on the unfixed script, including\n`expected exactly one docker-root walk per run, got ['-sx --block-size=1 /var/lib/docker',\n'-sx --block-size=1 /var/lib/docker/overlay2']`. The 12th is a ratchet asserting no wall-clock\nbound bypasses `clamped`, so the composition hole cannot be quietly reopened.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788636581048673587,"uuid":"12255f43-61cb-4ba5-a502-1871d5f105ec"}